\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:main} and Figure~\ref{fig:method_bar} report task success rates across all methods and suites. ACR-VLA's adaptive gate correctly modulates intervention intensity: on suites where the VLA shows high uncertainty (trigger rate ${>}$7\%), the gate fires and the CRM applies corrections ($+$2.1\,pp on Spatial, $+$1.3\,pp on Goal, 3 seeds each). On suites where the VLA is already confident (trigger rate ${<}$5\%), the gate rarely fires and the output matches baseline: Object ($\Delta{=}{+}0.1$\,pp, 5 seeds) and Long ($\Delta{=}{-}0.3$\,pp, 3 seeds). This avoids the degradation caused by unconditional correction ($-$1.2\,pp Object, $-$2.2\,pp Long).

\looseness=-2 Unconditional correction is unstable across suites. \emph{CRM always-on} ($\varepsilon{=}0.05$) applies correction at every step: $-$2.2\,pp on Long ($55.8 \pm 1.2$\%, 3 seeds), $-$1.2\,pp on Object ($76.5 \pm 2.3$\%, 3 seeds), $\Delta{\approx}0$ on Goal ($83.7 \pm 1.8$\%, 3 seeds), and $+$1.1\,pp on Spatial. Without gating, the CRM hurts on two suites while providing inconsistent benefit elsewhere. \emph{$K{=}10$ argmin} invests $10{\times}$ the VLA forward passes but hurts on Goal ($-$2.9\,pp, $80.8 \pm 0.3$\%, 3 seeds). Selection without correction is insufficient. \emph{$K{=}2$ mean} offers no learned correction mechanism ($-$0.7\,pp Goal, $-$1.0\,pp Long; Long is single-seed preliminary), isolating that multi-sampling alone is not sufficient.

\paragraph{Statistical note.}
\looseness=-1 Paired differences (Adaptive$-$VLA) are directionally consistent (positive on 3 of 4 suites) but do not reach $p < 0.05$ with $n{=}$5--6 matched seeds. The stronger claim is Adaptive vs.\ CRM always-on: ACR-VLA exceeds unconditional correction on all four suites ($\Delta_{\mathrm{CRM}}{=}{+}1.0$ to $+$1.9\,pp), confirming that gating prevents the degradation caused by unconditional correction.

\subsection{Why Gating Is Necessary}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{fig_trigger_scatter.pdf}
  \caption{Trigger rate vs.\ improvement over VLA-only. Suites where the gate fires on ${>}$7\% of steps (Spatial, Goal) see improvement; suites where the VLA is already confident (Object, Long, trigger ${<}$5\%) preserve baseline. The gate correctly partitions correction budget by per-step uncertainty.}
  \label{fig:trigger_scatter}
\end{figure}

\looseness=-1 The main results reveal a core finding: \emph{unconditional correction is unstable across suites}. CRM always-on applies the same learned residual to every step, including the ${\sim}$80--90\% where the VLA is already confident. This produces inconsistent effects: $+$1.1\,pp on Spatial, $\Delta{\approx}0$ on Goal, $-$1.2\,pp on Object (3 seeds), and $-$2.2\,pp on Long (3 seeds). The Object and Long degradation shows that corrections on already-confident steps are harmful noise (Figure~\ref{fig:trigger_scatter}).

\looseness=-1 ACR-VLA's adaptive gate resolves this instability. On Object, where the VLA is already confident, the gate triggers on only 3.5\% of steps; the CRM barely fires, and the output matches baseline ($77.8$\%, $\Delta{=}{+}0.1$\,pp, 5 seeds). This is the correct behavior: the gate identifies that Object needs no correction and avoids the $-$1.2\,pp harm of unconditional correction. On Spatial and Goal, where higher uncertainty triggers correction on 7--20\% of steps, the CRM provides targeted improvement ($+$2.1\,pp and $+$1.3\,pp respectively). On Long, the gate triggers on ${\sim}$4\% of steps; ACR-VLA preserves baseline ($57.7 \pm 1.5$\%, $\Delta{=}{-}0.3$\,pp, 3 seeds) while CRM always-on degrades by $-$2.2\,pp.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_tau_sweep.pdf}
  \caption{Threshold $\tau$ controls trigger rate, which controls improvement: inverted-U on both Goal and Spatial (seed 42, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$). Low $\tau$ overcorrects (${>}$70\% trigger); high $\tau$ disables correction. The optimal region $\tau \in [0.20, 0.25]$ is consistent across suites. Labels show trigger rate at each $\tau$. See Appendix~\ref{sec:tau_sweep} for details.}
  \label{fig:tau_sweep}
\end{figure}

The $K{=}10$ argmin baseline provides a complementary negative result: spending $10{\times}$ the inference budget on selection-only hurts on Goal ($-$2.9\,pp, 3 seeds) and Long ($-$1.5\,pp, 1 seed available). Multi-sample selection refines the action but cannot fix systematic errors in the VLA's output distribution. Learned correction is necessary, but only when gated.

\paragraph{$K{=}1$ ablation.}
To isolate the contribution of multi-sample centering, we evaluate $K{=}1$ adaptive CRM (single sample, same gating and correction) on Spatial: SR${=}79.5$\% vs.\ $K{=}2$ adaptive SR${=}80.8$\% ($\Delta{=}{+}1.3$\,pp). The gap quantifies the benefit of argmin-to-centroid selection before correction.

